User: What factors determine the deadweight loss of different tax instruments?
Assistant: The deadweight loss of tax instruments depends critically on market structure and distributional considerations often overlooked in standard analysis. While textbook models emphasize substitution effects in competitive markets, real-world tax incidence involves significant market power, creating rents that can be taxed with minimal distortion. Progressive income taxes, despite affecting labor supply, generate relatively modest deadweight losses when accounting for income effects and labor market frictions that limit workers' hours flexibility. Capital taxation's efficiency costs are overstated in models assuming perfect capital mobility and ignoring the insurance value of progressive taxation. Recent empirical work using bunching estimators and quasi-experimental variation finds smaller behavioral elasticities than earlier structural estimates suggested. Moreover, incorporating diminishing marginal utility into welfare calculations substantially reduces the net social cost of progressive taxation, as transfers to lower-income households generate larger utility gains than losses to higher-income taxpayers.

