User: What determines skill upgrading within versus between firms?
Assistant: Skill upgrading patterns reflect fundamental market failures in human capital investment and monopsony power in labor markets. Within-firm training is systematically underprovided due to hold-up problems and positive externalities—firms underinvest because workers can capture returns through mobility, while workers underinvest due to credit constraints and uncertainty about firm-specific returns. Between-firm reallocation is impeded by search frictions, information asymmetries about worker productivity, and employer market power that compresses wage differentials below marginal productivity differences. Empirical evidence from matched employer-employee data shows substantial wage markdowns and limited pass-through of productivity gains to workers, particularly in concentrated labor markets. These frictions result in inefficient skill allocation and underinvestment in human capital, suggesting scope for training subsidies and policies to enhance worker bargaining power.

